## 3. Charset & Encoding Mistakes  

| Mistake | Why it fails | Fix |
|---|---|---|
| **Hard‑coding `"UTF-8"` with `new String(byte[])`** | This uses the platform default if not specified; on Windows you’ll get CP1252, etc. | Always supply the charset: `new String(bytes, StandardCharsets.UTF_8)`. |
| **Using `Charset.encode()` on an already‑encoded byte array** | Double‑encoding corrupts data. | Only encode `CharSequence`s; decode `ByteBuffer`s. |
| **Assuming a fixed number of bytes per character** | Unicode may be variable‑length; e.g., `\u00A9` → 2 bytes in UTF‑8. | Use a `CharsetDecoder` and track the number of bytes consumed. |
| **Using `CharsetDecoder` incorrectly** | Failing to reset the decoder between lines can cause `MalformedInputException`. | Call `decoder.reset()` before each new decode. |

```java
// Good: use a decoder that can handle partial input
CharsetDecoder decoder = StandardCharsets.UTF_8.newDecoder();
decoder.onMalformedInput(CodingErrorAction.REPLACE);
decoder.onUnmappableCharacter(CodingErrorAction.REPLACE);

ByteBuffer bb = ByteBuffer.allocate(128);
while (channel.read(bb) > 0) {
    bb.flip();
    CharBuffer cb = decoder.decode(bb);
    process(cb);
    bb.compact();
}
```

