## 4. Forgetting to Close Channels  

| Mistake | Why it fails | Fix |
|---|---|---|
| **Closing only the file, not the channel** | The underlying OS resource stays open until GC. | Use `try (FileChannel fc = FileChannel.open(...)) { … }`. |
| **Not closing a `Selector`** | The selector thread stays alive; memory leaks. | `selector.close()` or `try (Selector sel = Selector.open())`. |
| **Leaving a `FileLock` open** | The file becomes locked forever. | Call `lock.release()` or use try‑with‑resources. |

```java
try (FileChannel fc = FileChannel.open(path, StandardOpenOption.READ)) {
    // do I/O
} catch (IOException e) {
    // handle
}
```

---

## 5. Mixing Blocking & Non‑Blocking on the Same Channel  

| Mistake | Why it fails | Fix |
|---|---|---|
| **Calling `socketChannel.configureBlocking(false)` then using `channel.read()` as if it were blocking** | You’ll get `0` immediately and may misinterpret it as EOF. | In non‑blocking mode always use a `Selector` or loop until data arrives. |
| **Using `Thread.sleep()` to wait for data** | Wastes CPU, still possible to miss data. | Use `Selector` with `select(timeout)` or `selectNow()`. |

```java
// Good: non‑blocking read with selector
SocketChannel sc = SocketChannel.open();
sc.configureBlocking(false);
Selector sel = Selector.open();
sc.register(sel, SelectionKey.OP_READ);

while (true) {
    if (sel.select() > 0) {
        for (SelectionKey key : sel.selectedKeys()) {
            if (key.isReadable()) {
                SocketChannel ch = (SocketChannel) key.channel();
                ByteBuffer buf = ByteBuffer.allocate(1024);
                int n = ch.read(buf);
                // handle n
            }
        }
        sel.selectedKeys().clear();
    }
}
```

---

## 6. Misusing Selectors & SelectionKeys  

| Mistake | Why it fails | Fix |
|---|---|---|
| **Registering a key with only `OP_READ` and later trying to write** | The channel won’t be notified for write readiness. | Register all needed ops: `channel.register(selector, ops)`. |
| **Not cancelling keys on close** | Keys linger in the selector, causing memory leaks. | `key.cancel();` in finally block. |
| **Calling `key.interestOps()` incorrectly** | Overwriting the previous ops or forgetting to include existing ones. | `key.interestOps(key.interestOps() | SelectionKey.OP_WRITE);` |
| **Using `SelectionKey.isValid()` incorrectly** | A closed channel may still report valid if not cancelled. | Always check `key.isValid()` before using the channel. |

---

## 7. Direct Buffer Pitfalls  

| Mistake | Why it fails | Fix |
|---|---|---|
| **Allocating a large `ByteBuffer.allocateDirect()` and never releasing it** | The buffer lives outside the GC heap; the JVM may run out of native memory. | Use try‑with‑resources or a buffer pool; or manually clean with `((sun.nio.ch.DirectBuffer)buf).cleaner().clean()`. |
| **Treating a direct buffer as if it were heap‑based (e.g., calling `array()`)** | Throws `UnsupportedOperationException`. | Use `buf.get()` or `buf.position()`. |
| **Over‑allocating direct buffers for short operations** | Direct allocation is expensive. | Prefer `allocate()` for short or small buffers. |

```java
// Good: use a pool or manual clean
try (ByteBuffer buf = ByteBuffer.allocateDirect(8192)) {
    // I/O
} // no explicit clean needed; GC will handle it
```

---

## 8. File Channel Position & Random Access Errors  

| Mistake | Why it fails | Fix |
|---|---|---|
| **Using the same `position()` for both read & write without resetting** | The write may start in the middle of the read data. | Keep separate `readPos` and `writePos` or use `channel.position(newPos)` before each operation. |
| **Assuming `position()` will auto‑increment** | It stays at the last set position. | Do `channel.read(buf, pos)` or `channel.write(buf, pos)` to specify an explicit offset. |
| **Using `truncate(0)` to “clear” a file, then writing without repositioning** | You’ll write at position 0, but the channel may still hold an old position. | After truncate, call `channel.position(0)` or simply `newFileChannel` again. |

```java
// Good: explicit positions
long readPos = 0;
long writePos = 0;
ByteBuffer buf = ByteBuffer.allocate(256);

while (channel.read(buf, readPos) != -1) {
    buf.flip();
    channel.write(buf, writePos);
    writePos += buf.limit();
    readPos += buf.limit();
    buf.clear();
}
```

